---
// firma2-rail-section — a section INSIDE the record rail: a title, an optional
// action beside it, and content. The rail's answer to what esa-card is in the
// main column.
//
// WHY THE RAIL'S SECTIONS ARE NOT CARDS. They were, and the card was doing real
// work while these sat in a scrolling column: a border is what says where one
// section ends and the next begins when there is nothing else to say it. In a
// rail there IS something else — the rail is its own column, ruled off from the
// content by a hairline down its leading edge, so every card inside it drew a
// second box immediately inside the first. (The rail was a filled surface when
// that was first written and has since taken the content pane's own background;
// the argument did not depend on the fill, only on the column being already
// bounded, which the rule still does.) Two nested containers with one step of
// padding between them is the boxes-inside-boxes read that firma2-card-section
// already deleted one line to avoid, and here it was a whole extra border on
// every section. The rail says "this column is the record". The sections inside
// only have to say where each one starts, and a title plus a step of space does
// that. Polish is subtractive.
//
// WHAT IS LOST, HONESTLY: the card's surface, which lifted the section off the
// page, and its padding, which held content clear of its edge. Neither is needed
// in here — the rail supplies both, once, for the column.
//
// THE HEADING KEEPS THE CARD'S TYPOGRAPHY ROLE. `typography-title-sm-strong` is
// what esa-card renders its own title at, and an <h3> is what it rendered, so
// dropping the card changes nothing about the document outline or the type
// scale. A reader diffing the two versions should see one box disappear, not a
// section re-typeset.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: esa-card IS the lego for this shape and it is the right lego
// in the main column — this page's other four sections still compose it, and
// this component is deliberately NOT a re-implementation of it (no surface, no
// border, no elevation, no padding; it has none of the things a card is). What
// is left after removing all of those is a heading and a slot, which is not a
// card with the styling turned off, it is a different object. Walked the catalog
// for a section/heading primitive that ships without a container — esa-card is
// the only sectioning lego and its `variant` prop controls fill and elevation,
// not whether the box exists. Beacon is not cloned on this machine. The layout
// is `.stack` + `.repel`, both hub primitives; nothing here is hand-rolled flex.
import type { HTMLAttributes } from 'astro/types';

interface Props extends HTMLAttributes<'section'> {
  /** The section's name. Renders as the <h3>, at esa-card's own title role. */
  title: string;
  /**
   * Keep the heading in the document and take it off the screen.
   *
   * FOR ONE CASE ONLY: the section is inside a TAB, and the tab's label is
   * already the section's name in the reader's eye. Rendering both puts
   * "Contacts" twice on one 22rem column, one line apart — the redundancy
   * design-principles cuts. Deleting the heading instead would be the wrong
   * trade: esa-tab-layout's panel is not associated with its tab (no
   * aria-labelledby — a real lego gap), so the heading is the only thing naming
   * this region in the heading list a screen-reader user navigates by. Hidden,
   * it costs a sighted reader nothing and keeps that name.
   */
  titleHidden?: boolean;
}

const { title, titleHidden = false, class: className, ...rest } = Astro.props;

// a11y.css's clip-rect pattern, global from @esa/tokens. Not `display: none`,
// which would take the heading out of the accessibility tree — the one thing
// this mode exists to keep.
const titleClass = [
  'firma2-rail-section__title',
  'typography-title-sm-strong',
  titleHidden && 'visually-hidden',
]
  .filter(Boolean)
  .join(' ');

// The action row only renders when a page actually passes one. An empty .repel
// is a zero-width flex box, so this is not about layout — it is about not
// emitting a wrapper that describes a control that is not there.
const hasActions = Astro.slots.has('actions');
---
<!-- A NAMED <section>. Unlike the map's grouping element, this one takes its
     accessible name from the heading inside it via aria-labelledby... which it
     cannot do without an id, and an id would have to be unique per instance.
     The <h3> is enough on its own: a heading inside a plain <section> already
     gives a screen reader the section's name in the heading list, which is how
     a reader navigates a rail of short sections. No landmark, deliberately —
     the rail is one <aside> and stacking two complementary regions inside it
     would make the landmark list longer without making it more useful. -->
<section class:list={['firma2-rail-section', 'stack', className]} data-gap="sm" {...rest}>
  {hasActions ? (
    <!-- `.repel` is the primitive for exactly this pair — a title pushed away
         from its actions, wrapping when cramped. It is what the rail needs
         instead of a card header: same two things, same arrangement, no band and
         no rule under it. -->
    <div class="firma2-rail-section__head repel">
      <h3 class={titleClass}>{title}</h3>
      <slot name="actions" />
    </div>
  ) : (
    <h3 class={titleClass}>{title}</h3>
  )}

  <slot />
</section>

<style>
  /* The <h3>'s UA margin, and nothing else. Every other property the title needs
     arrives on the composite class in the markup — esa-card's own title role, so
     the type is identical to the sections in the main column. */
  .firma2-rail-section__title {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* No rules for the section itself: `.stack` spaces the title from the content
     and the rail's own stack spaces one section from the next. A component whose
     entire layout comes from two primitives is the goal, not an omission. */
</style>
